  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-109289",
    "knowledge_point_id": "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f",
    "knowledge_point": "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement",
    "problem": "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$",
    "worked_solution": "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}} & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2 & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & <-2 \\\\ t & <{e}^{-2} \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).$",
